Step 4: Clean the text
python
import re

def clean(text):
    text = re.sub(r"[-\u2010]\n(\w)", r"\1", text)   # rejoin words split at line ends
    text = re.sub(r"[ \t]+", " ", text)              # collapse repeated spaces
    text = re.sub(r"\n{3,}", "\n\n", text)           # collapse extra blank lines
    return text.strip()

cleaned_pages = []
for p in pages:
    t = clean(p["text"])
    if len(t) >= 50:                                 # drop blank pages
        cleaned_pages.append({"page": p["page"], "text": t})

print("Pages kept:", len(cleaned_pages))

The book uses a special hyphen (\u2010) at line ends, about 736 times, so the pattern includes it. A plain - alone would miss most of them. The small side effect is that a genuine hyphenated word broken at a line end, such as “state-of-the-art”, also gets joined. It’s minor, and you can mention it as a limitation.

